Well, I would not say that the Quest is horrible, Master Kaen is
horrible.  But, with planning, the right tools, the sleep spell and Pw
>= 100 (or an equivalent) it is possible to keep him perpetually
frozen.  In all of the games where I faced Master Kaen (4 or 5 or 6)
during this project I was down to about Pw=0 by the time that I
actually killed him.

As for why monks, my biggest draw to monks was having the sleeping
spell.  This spell effectively neutralized team q, n, and a, which are
early game hazards and also was crucial to taking on Master Kaen.  The
other big advantage to the sleep spell is the ability to acquire
unspent wands and potions from dungeon denizens (like leprechauns who
are generated asleep).  While asleep, monsters can't zap their wands
or drink potions, which means that I get them.  I spammed sleep quite
heavily against most anything not only to gather their treasure but
also just in case they had a nasty wand.  One of my deaths would have
been prevented if I could have gotten a sleep spell off before being
zapped with a wand of death.

The other things that Jaina mentioned were of course quite handy,
too.  Weaponless combat of course favors monks and samurai but really
nothing beats a pet purple worm or a cockatrice corpse.  Not wearing
armor was kind of annoying but I did find once a black dragon scale
mail that (had I survived) would have considered wearing.  But mostly
I just stayed out of fights or, again, let my pet handle them. Like
Jaina said, the quest artifact is powerful.  I found that acquiring MR
was crucial and without the luck of finding a good bones file or cloak
of MR lying around you're going to lose polyselfless eventually unless
you want to give up a ring or amulet slot (and have the necessary ring
and/or amulet).  Intrinsics are important and although I have ascended
without cold or fire resistance, it takes some effort, and hunting
gelatinous cubes takes some work.

I should also mention that mapping the mazes (without scrolls or
spells) is so much easier with the Eyes of the Overworld.  Not really
crucial but certainly takes out some of the tedium.

